ch correction, target leakage), and (3) **training-time computational patterns** that will not scale. Below I give a rigorous, actionable critique and a concrete merged design \u2014 **PertNet-Cascade** \u2014 that fixes these issues.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**PertNet-Graph (DE proposal)**\n- \u2705 Sparse GRN propagation is the right inductive bias for CRISPRi.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Bottleneck**: The \"latent dynamical system\" is under-specified. If it's an ODE/RNN over genes, it's O(N_genes\u00b2) per step and will not scale to 3,000 HVGs \u00d7 8k perturbations. If it's a transformer over genes, attention is O(N_genes\u00b2) memory.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Missing**: No explicit handling of **CRISPRi partial knockdown** (70\u201390% efficiency). A \"soft deletion\" embedding without a knockdown-efficiency scalar will systematically over-predict effect size.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Residual decoder anchored to baseline** is good, but the anchor must\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert Feedback: BioPert-Cascade & PertNet-Cascade\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nBoth proposals are strong, but from a **single-cell biology** perspective they still under-model several mechanisms that dominate CRISPRi K562 biology and will determine whether the model actually beats GEARS/CPA on unseen perturbations. My critique focuses on: (1) **CRISPRi is not knockout** \u2014 partial knockdown, transcript-level vs protein-level effects, and dCas9-KRAB-specific artifacts must be explicit; (2) **K562 is a near-haploid, highly aneuploid, erythroleukemic line** with a specific GRN (GATA1/TAL1/KLF1, heme biosynthesis, BCR-ABL1-driven signaling) \u2014 generic STRING/Reactome priors will dilute signal; (3) **indirect effects dominate** \u2014 most measured DE genes after CRISPRi are 2\u20133 hops downstream, so a shallow GNN or single-hop propagation will systematically miss the biology; (4) **cell-state heterogeneity within K562** (cell cycle, erythroid differentiation drift) is a major confounder that neither proposal conditions on explicitly enough. I propose a consolidated **BioPert-Cascade v2** with concrete architectural fixes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**PertNet-Graph (DE)**\n- \u2705 Sparse GRN is the correct inductive bias.\n- \u274c **Latent dynamical system is biologically wrong as specified.** CRISPRi steady-state DE at ~72\u201396h is not a dynamical trajectory we can integrate; it is a *fixed point* of a regulatory network with partial knockdown. An\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer Critical Questions: Driving Toward a SOTA-Surpassing Design\n\nBuilding on the three-way discussion (PertNet-Graph \u2192 BioPert-Cascade \u2192 PertNet-Cascade), I want to sharpen the remaining ambiguities into **actionable engineering questions**. My focus is on the questions whose answers will most directly determine whether the merged design actually beats GEARS/CPA on the specified metrics (MSE, PCC, R\u00b2, Pearson) under K562 CRISPRi sparsity.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Clarifications \u2014 Architecture & Implementation\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 What is the *exact* parameterization of the knockdown-efficiency scalar, and is it identifiable?**\nBoth BioPert-Cascade and my critique flagged CRISPRi's 70\u201390% efficiency. But *how* is `efficiency(target)` learned vs. fixed? Options:\n- (a) Per-target learnable scalar (fails on unseen targets \u2014 the exact generalization case we care about),\n- (b) Predicted from target gene features (essentiality, expression, chromatin accessibility),\n- (c) A single global scalar with per-target residual.\n\n**This is the single most important design decision for unseen-perturbation generalization.** I need the Single Cell Biology Expert to specify which, and the Cellforge context to tell us whether the dataset provides measured knockdown efficiency per target (some CRISPRi screens do via qPCR/RNA-seq of the target itself).\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 How does the \"three-tier latent hierarchy\" (gene \u2192 module \u2192 state) actually share information without leaking?**\nIf module-level latents are supervised by pathway labels AND gene-level latents feed the decoder, we risk **information leakage\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert Response: Consolidating BioPert-Cascade v2 with Answers to the Data Engineer's Critical Questions\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe Data Engineer's questions are exactly the right ones \u2014 they target the identifiability and leakage issues that separate a \"biologically plausible\" design from one that actually **generalizes to unseen CRISPRi targets in K562**. I'll answer each question with concrete, biologically-motivated parameterizations, then consolidate the final architecture. My core position: **the model must be a structured causal graph with partial-knockdown physics, not a learned black-box shift**, and the identifiability constraints must be baked in via architecture (not just loss terms).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Answers to the Data Engineer's Critical Questions\n\n### Q1.1 \u2014 Parameterization of knockdown efficiency `\u03b7(target)`\n\n**Answer: (b) with (c) as fallback \u2014 a predicted efficiency head with a global prior.**\n\nThe biological reasoning:\n\n1. **Per-target learnable scalars (a) are disqualified** \u2014 they cannot generalize to unseen targets, which is the exact evaluation regime. This is the failure mode of GEARS.\n\n2. **CRISPRi efficiency is *predictable* from features we already have in K562**:\n   - **Target expression level** (highly expressed genes \u2192 more mRNA to knock down \u2192 lower fractional depletion at fixed dCas9-KRAB occupancy; empirically \u03b7 decreases with expression).\n   - **Promoter chromatin accessibility** (ATAC-seq available for K562 in ENCODE) \u2014 dCas9-KRAB requires accessible chromatin.\n   - **Distance of sgRNA target site from TSS** (if sgRNA sequences are provided;\nCritic: # Critical Evaluation of BioPert-Cascade / PertNet-Cascade for Single-Cell CRISPRi Perturbation Prediction\n\n## Preamble\n\nThe three-way dialogue (Single Cell Biology Expert, Data Engineer, and prior proposals) has produced an unusually mature design. My job as Critic is not to rubber-stamp it, but to stress-test whether the consolidated **BioPert-Cascade v2 / PertNet-Cascade** actually has a plausible path to beating GEARS/CPA on the specified metrics (MSE, PCC, R\u00b2, Pearson) under K562 CRISPRi sparsity \u2014 and to flag where the design risks over-engineering itself into an unvalidatable state.\n\nThe evaluation below is deliberately skeptical. A design this elaborate carries real risk of **theoretical elegance masking empirical fragility**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scientific Merit \u2014 **Score: 0.82**\n\n**Theoretical foundation (strong).** The reframing of CRISPRi steady-state DE as a *fixed point of a partially-perturbed regulatory network* rather than a dynamical trajectory is biologically correct and is a genuine conceptual advance over CPA/scGen shift-vector learning. The three-tier latent hierarchy (gene \u2192 module \u2192 state) maps cleanly onto known biology (direct targets \u2192 pathway modules \u2192 cell-state manifestation) and gives the model a principled inductive bias.\n\n**Methodological rigor (good, with gaps).** The identifiability constraint separating direct vs. indirect effects is the right move, but the proposal is vague on *how* it is enforced. If it is only a loss term, it will be dominated by the reconstruction objective at K562 sparsity (~100\u2013200 cells/perturbation). The Data Engineer correctly flagged this\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\n\nKey Data Engineering Considerations:\n1. Data Processing:\n   - Quality control metrics and thresholds\n   - Normalization methods for single-cell data\n   - Feature selection strategies\n   - Batch effect correction\n\n2. Data Integration:\n   - Multi-omics data integration\n   - Cross-dataset harmonization\n   - Metadata incorporation\n   - External knowledge base integration\n\n3. Computational Efficiency:\n   - Scalable data structures\n   - Efficient matrix operations\n   - Memory optimization\n   - Parallel processing\n\n4. Data Quality:\n   - Missing value handling\n   - Noise reduction\n   - Outlier detection\n   - Data validation procedures\nPlease propose a detailed architectural solution focusing on your domain expertise. Your proposal should aim to surpass current SOTA models in single-cell perturbation prediction.\n\nRequired sections:\n\n1. Architecture Components:\n   - Detailed description of each component\n   - Configuration parameters and their justification\n   - How each component addresses specific challenges in single-cell data\n   - Integration points with other components\n\n2. Biological Considerations:\n   - How the architecture handles cell type specificity\n   - Incorporation of pathway knowledge\n   - Mechanisms for capturing perturbation effects\n   - Biological constraints and their implementation\n\n3. Technical Implementation:\n   - Detailed layer specifications\n   - Activation functions and their biological relevance\n   - Loss function design\n   - Training strategy recommendations\n\n4. Performance Optimization:\n   - Specific techniques to outperform SOTA\n   - Computational efficiency considerations\n   - Scalability solutions\n   - Memory optimization strategies\n\n5. Integration and Interpretability:\n   - Methods for model interpretation\n   - Visualization approaches\n   - Integration with existing biological knowledge\n   - Validation strategies\n\nFormat your response as a structured proposal with clear sections and specific technical details."
