8\u201310k perturbations \u00d7 ~100\u2013200 cells each, over ~2\u20133k HVGs. Any per-perturbation parameterization is unidentifiable; the model must be **target-conditioned, not target-parameterized**.\n2. **Leakage risk**: Target gene expression is *both* a feature and a label. Any pipeline that normalizes, selects features, or imputes using the perturbed cells will silently inflate PCC.\n3. **Batch confounding**: CRISPRi screens are run in batches (guides, days, lanes). If batch is not modeled as a *first-class* nuisance variable, the model will learn batch\u2192expression shortcuts that look like perturbation signal on held-out guides from the same batch.\n\nThe architecture below is organized around a **strict data contract** (Section 0) that every component must obey, then the model components (Sections 1\u20135). The data contract is the single most important contribution from the data-engineering side, because it is what makes the biological claims testable.\n\n---\n\n## Section 0 \u2014 Data Contract (Prerequisite to All Components)\n\nEvery downstream component assumes the following pipeline. This is not optional\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # BioPert-Cascade v3: A Biologically-Grounded Architecture for K562 CRISPRi Perturbation Prediction\n\n## Preamble: Design Philosophy\n\nThe preceding dialogue has converged on three non-negotiable truths about this problem:\n\n1. **CRISPRi is partial knockdown at a fixed point**, not a knockout trajectory \u2014 the model must encode knockdown physics, not shift vectors.\n2. **Indirect effects dominate** measured DE genes \u2014 the architecture must propagate signal through a multi-hop regulatory graph, not a single-hop shift.\n3. **Per-perturbation parameters are unidentifiable** at ~100\u2013200 cells/perturbation \u2014 every component must be *target-conditioned via features*, never target-parameterized.\n\nMy contribution here is the **biological substrate** that binds these truths into a coherent, interpretable, and trainable model. I organize the response around the required sections, but the centerpiece is a **K562-specific, CRISPRi-specific causal graph** with a **partial-knockdown fixed-point solver** at its core.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture Components\n\n### 1.1 Overview\n\nBioPert-Cascade v3 is a **five-module cascade**:\n\n```\n[Input: (cell state x, target gene t, sgRNA features)]\n        \u2502\n        \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 M1. State Encoder (cell-context embedding)  \u2502  \u2190 cell cycle + erythroid drift\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n        \u2502\n        \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 M2. Knockdown Physics Head \u03b7(t, x)          \u2502  \u2190\nData Engineer: # Data Engineering Review of BioPert-Cascade v3 & PertNet-Cascade v3\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nBoth proposals are biologically sophisticated and architecturally coherent, but from a **data-engineering standpoint** they share a critical structural weakness: **the data contract (Section 0) is asserted, not specified**, and the biological components (M2 knockdown physics, multi-hop graph propagation) impose data-shape and numerical-stability requirements that the current proposals do not resolve. Below I give a technical critique focused on what will actually determine whether these models beat SOTA on K562 CRISPRi, and concrete fixes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**Strengths**\n- Target-conditioned (not target-parameterized) design is correct for the ~100\u2013200 cells/perturbation regime. This is the single most important architectural decision and both proposals get it right.\n- The fixed-point solver for partial knockdown is the right inductive bias \u2014 CRISPRi does not produce `x - \u0394`; it produces a new equilibrium of a regulatory ODE. Encoding this as a *solver* rather than a *shift* is a genuine differentiator vs. GEARS/scGPT-style baselines.\n\n**Critical weaknesses**\n\n1. **Fixed-point solver convergence is unspecified and is a hard blocker.** A regulatory ODE fixed-point solver inside a training loop requires (a) guaranteed contraction (spectral radius < 1 of the Jacobian), (b) a differentiability strategy (implicit differentiation vs. unrolled), and (c) a fixed iteration budget. Neither proposal specifies the **contraction guarantee**. Without it, training will either diverge or be dominated by solver overhead\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert Feedback: BioPert-Cascade v3 & PertNet-Cascade v3\n\n## Preamble\n\nBoth proposals are biologically literate and architecturally serious. They correctly reject the naive \"shift vector\" paradigm and correctly insist on target-conditioning over target-parameterization. But from a **single-cell biology** standpoint, three biological facts dominate K562 CRISPRi and neither proposal fully exploits them:\n\n1. **K562 is a highly aneuploid, near-haploid-ish CML blast line with a small, well-characterized core regulatory network** (GATA1/TAL1/KLF1 erythroid; SPI1 myeloid; MYB, MYC, CEBPA). This is a *feature*, not a nuisance \u2014 the effective regulatory graph is small (~200\u2013400 TFs), dense, and heavily literature-annotated. Both proposals build a \"multi-hop graph\" but neither commits to **K562-specific prior graph curation** as a first-class component.\n2. **CRISPRi in K562 produces strong on-target knockdown (often 70\u201390%) but the downstream DE is dominated by a handful of *modules*, not individual genes.** The measured response is essentially a **module-level shift** with a sparse, low-rank structure. Neither proposal explicitly encodes this low-rank module structure, which is the single biggest lever for beating SOTA under sparsity.\n3. **The strongest confounders in K562 CRISPRi are cell-cycle state and erythroid differentiation drift**, both of which are continuous latent variables with well-known marker genes (CD71, CD235a, GATA1, CYCLINB1, PCNA). M1 \"state encoder\" is underspecified in both\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer Queries: Forcing the Proposals to Be Trainable, Leakage-Free, and SOTA-Competitive\n\n## Framing\n\nThe biology expert and I agree on the *what* (fixed-point knockdown physics, multi-hop propagation, low-rank module structure, continuous cell-state nuisance). My questions below are the **operational interrogations** that must be answered before either proposal can be implemented, benchmarked, and defended. They are grouped by the five required categories, and each question is paired with the concrete artifact I need from the team.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Clarifications\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Contraction guarantee for the fixed-point solver.** BioPert-Cascade v3 asserts a fixed-point solver for partial knockdown but does not specify the spectral-radius bound on the regulatory Jacobian. Which of the following do you commit to, and what is the *numerical* certificate?\n\n- (a) Explicit normalization: `W_reg \u2190 W_reg / (\u2016W_reg\u2016\u2082 + \u03b5)` each forward pass, guaranteeing `\u03c1(J) \u2264 1 \u2212 \u03b4`.\n- (b) Spectral normalization on each layer of the graph propagator.\n- (c) Residual parameterization `x* = x + \u03b1 \u00b7 f(x, t)` with `\u03b1 \u2208 (0,1)` and Lipschitz-bounded `f`.\n\n**Why I need this:** the solver runs inside every training step across ~8\u201310k perturbations \u00d7 100\u2013200 cells. Without a contraction certificate, (i) training diverges on some batches, (ii) implicit differentiation is invalid, (iii) wall-clock budget blows up. **Deliverable: a one-page proof sketch + a unit\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\n\nKey Biological Considerations:\n1. Cell Type Specificity:\n   - Cell type-specific response patterns\n   - Lineage relationships and developmental trajectories\n   - Cell state transitions and plasticity\n   - Tissue-specific regulatory networks\n\n2. Perturbation Response:\n   - Direct vs indirect effects\n   - Temporal dynamics of responses\n   - Compensatory mechanisms\n   - Pathway cross-talk\n\n3. Regulatory Networks:\n   - Transcription factor networks\n   - Signaling cascades\n   - Metabolic pathways\n   - Gene regulatory circuits\n\n4. Quality Control:\n   - Technical vs biological variation\n   - Batch effects and normalization\n   - Cell cycle effects\n   - Dropout patterns"
    }
