dvantage over GEARS (which learns per-gene perturbation embeddings that do not extrapolate to unseen targets) and\nData Engineer: # BioPert-Cascade v5: Data-Engineering Specification for a SOTA-Beating Single-Cell Perturbation Predictor\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nv4's biology is correct; v4's *data contract* is where it will lose to GEARS. This proposal specifies **v5** \u2014 a data-engineering-first refinement that keeps the fixed-point CRISPRi gate, low-rank module residual, and continuous cell-state nuisance, but **adds the four pipeline interventions that close the ~8\u201312% MSE gap** the audit identified, plus the training-time mechanics that make the fixed-point solver actually converge on K562's sparse-target regime.\n\nThe four load-bearing additions:\n\n1. **Target-Baseline Anchoring Tensor (TBAT)** \u2014 a leakage-audited, batch-matched scalar covariate on `\u03ba_t` that recovers GEARS' signal without leaking target identity.\n2. **Sparse-Target Curriculum Scheduler** \u2014 a training-time sampler that ramps from dense to sparse targets so the fixed-point solver converges on rare perturbations.\n3. **K562-Stratified Evaluation Protocol** \u2014 batch-matched, target-disjoint splits with per-target variance weighting, so reported wins are real.\n4. **Deterministic Preprocessing Contract** \u2014 a versioned, hash-pinned pipeline (QC \u2192 normalization \u2192 feature selection \u2192 batch correction \u2192 covariate extraction) that makes the model reproducible and the comparison to baselines fair.\n\nEverything below is written as a **data-engineering contract**: tensor shapes, preprocessing steps, sampler logic, loss terms, and evaluation splits. Where the biology spec constrains a choice, I cite it; where the pipeline constrains the biology, I flag it.\n\n---\n\n## \n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\n\nKey Biological Considerations:\n1. Cell Type Specificity:\n   - Cell type-specific response patterns\n   - Lineage relationships and developmental trajectories