2) the failure modes, (3) concrete v6 additions, (4) feasibility and risk.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What v5 Gets Right (Preserve These)\n\n- **TBAT anchoring with leakage audit** \u2014 this is the single highest-leverage addition. GEARS' advantage on sparse targets comes largely from target-specific baseline statistics; TBAT recovers that signal while the leakage audit keeps it defensible. **Do not touch this.**\n- **Sparse-target curriculum** \u2014 correct diagnosis. The fixed-point solver diverges on targets with <20 cells in K562 CRISPRi; curriculum is the right lever.\n- **Stratified eval protocol with per-target variance weighting** \u2014 this is what makes the \"we beat SOTA\" claim honest. Keep it.\n- **Deterministic, hash-pinned preprocessing** \u2014 non-negotiable. Any comparison to GEARS without this is meaningless.\n- **Module-level regulatory topology prior (SCENIC-style)** \u2014 biologically correct and sample-efficient. This is v5's best new idea.\n- **Sign-constrained compensatory head** \u2014 correct\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert \u2014 Critical Review of v5 and Specification for v6\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nv5 is biologically the strongest proposal in the thread, but as the domain expert responsible for **whether the biology it encodes is actually correct and actually learnable from K562 CRISPRi data**, I have to flag that v5 has **two biological correctness errors**, **one interpretability overclaim**, and **one missed opportunity that is the single largest remaining source of MSE gap versus GEARS**. The Data Engineer's failure modes (convergence, sparsity, leakage) are real but they are *downstream* of these \u2014 if the biology is wrong, no amount of curriculum scheduling rescues the MSE.\n\nMy review is organized as: (1) what v5 gets biologically right and must be preserved, (2) the two correctness errors, (3) the missed opportunity (this is where the SOTA gap actually lives), (4) concrete v6 specification, (5) feasibility and risk.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What v5 Gets Biologically Right (Preserve)\n\n- **Multiplicative CRISPRi gate in a fixed-point solver.** This is the correct mechanism. CRISPRi knockdown is a multiplicative attenuation of the target's *effective transcription rate*, not an additive shift in expression. The fixed-point formulation correctly captures that indirect effects propagate through the regulatory network until they settle. Do not change this.\n- **SCENIC-style module topology prior.** This is v5's best idea and the primary source of sample efficiency. K562 has a well-characterized regulon structure (GATA1/2, TAL1, KLF1 erythroid program; MYC, MAX, E2F proliferative program).\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer \u2014 Critical Questions to Drive v6 to SOTA\n\nThe v5 discussion has converged on biology (multiplicative CRISPRi gate + fixed-point solver) and a data contract (TBAT, curriculum, stratified eval). Before I can commit engineering resources to a v6 spec, I need concrete answers on **eight** load-bearing questions. Each is framed so that a wrong answer is a silent failure mode, not a loud one.\n\n---\n\n## 1. TBAT Leakage Audit \u2014 What Exactly Is the Leakage Criterion?\n\nThe Single Cell Biology Expert and I both endorse TBAT, but \"leakage-audited\" is currently a slogan, not a spec. I need:\n\n- **Precise definition of the leakage boundary.** Is TBAT computed from (a) the *control* cells of the same target's batch, (b) the *global* control distribution, or (c) the *perturbed* cells themselves? If (c), we have target-identity leakage and the \"generalize to unseen perturbations\" objective is violated by construction.\n- **Batch-matching protocol.** K562 CRISPRi data is almost certainly multi-batch (multiple screens, multiple guides per target). How is TBAT computed so that batch effects in the baseline don't masquerade as target-specific signal? Concretely: does TBAT use per-batch control means, or a batch-corrected pooled control mean?\n- **Unseen-target behavior.** For a target with *zero* cells in the training set, what is TBAT's value? If it defaults to the global mean, we've thrown away the anchoring signal precisely where we need it. If it's predicted from\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # BioPert-Cascade v6: Final Integrated Architecture Specification\n\n## Single Cell Biology Expert \u2014 Closing the SOTA Gap on K562 CRISPRi\n\n---\n\n## Preamble: What v6 Resolves\n\nThe thread has converged on a coherent mechanism (multiplicative CRISPRi gate + fixed-point solver), a coherent data contract (TBAT, curriculum, stratified eval), and a coherent biological prior (SCENIC regulon topology). My job is to deliver the **final v6 specification** that:\n\n1. **Answers the Data Engineer's eight load-bearing questions** with concrete, implementable answers \u2014 not slogans.\n2. **Corrects the two biological errors** I flagged in v5 (compensatory head sign prior was too strong; SCENIC prior was applied at the wrong granularity).\n3. **Closes the largest remaining MSE gap vs. GEARS** \u2014 which, as I argued, is *not* in the pipeline but in the **missed opportunity: paralog-level compensatory buffering**. This is where the SOTA gap actually lives on K562 CRISPRi.\n\nI will be explicit: **the single biggest win in v6 is the Paralog Compensation Module (PCM)**. Everything else is refinement. If we implement only one thing from this document, it is PCM.\n\n---\n\n## Section 1 \u2014 What v5 Gets Right (Preserved Unchanged)\n\n- **Multiplicative CRISPRi gate in a fixed-point solver.** Correct mechanism. Do not change.\n- **TBAT with leakage audit.** Highest-leverage data addition. Preserved, with the leakage criterion now *specified* (Section 4).\n- **Sparse-target curriculum scheduler.** Correct lever. Preserved\nCritic: # Critic's Comprehensive Evaluation: BioPert-Cascade v5/v6 Proposal Series\n\n## Preamble: What I Am Evaluating\n\nI am evaluating the **v5 \u2192 v6 proposal arc** as documented across three expert contributions (Single Cell Biology Expert, Data Engineer, and the integrated v6 spec). My evaluation is grounded in the stated design goals: (1) beat SOTA on gene_knockout in K562, (2) biological interpretability, (3) generalization under sparsity, (4) computational efficiency. I score against the **evaluation metrics actually specified in the task** (MSE, PCC, R2, Pearson) \u2014 not against the proposal's own internal narrative.\n\nA critical framing note before scoring: **the proposal has strong internal coherence but has not yet produced a single number against a baseline.** Every claim of \"beats GEARS\" is a hypothesis, not a result. My scores reflect *design quality and probability of delivering*, not demonstrated performance. I weight this heavily \u2014 a beautiful architecture that cannot be validated is worth less than a modest one that clears the bar.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scientific Merit \u2014 **Score: 0.78**\n\n**Theoretical foundation (strong, 0.85):** The multiplicative CRISPRi gate inside a fixed-point solver is the correct mechanistic model. CRISPRi attenuates effective transcription rate; indirect effects propagate through the regulatory network until equilibrium. This is a genuine advance over GEARS' additive latent-shift formulation, which is mechanistically wrong for knockdowns (it treats perturbation as a translation in embedding space rather than an attenuation of a rate). The v6 correction \u2014 reducing the compensatory head's sign constraint from hard to soft, and\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\n\nKey Data Engineering Considerations:\n1. Data Processing:\n   - Quality control metrics and thresholds\n   - Normalization methods for single-cell data\n   - Feature selection strategies\n   - Batch effect correction\n\n2. Data Integration:\n   - Multi-omics data integration\n   - Cross-dataset harmonization\n   - Metadata incorporation\n   - External knowledge base integration\n\n3. Computational Efficiency:\n   - Scalable data structures\n   - Efficient matrix operations\n   - Memory optimization\n   - Parallel processing\n\n4. Data Quality:\n   - Missing value handling\n   - Noise reduction\n   - Outlier detection\n   - Data validation procedures\nPlease propose a detailed architectural solution focusing on your domain expertise. Your proposal should aim to surpass current SOTA models in single-cell perturbation prediction.\n\nRequired sections:\n\n1. Architecture Components:\n   - Detailed description of each component\n   - Configuration parameters and their justification\n   - How each component addresses specific challenges in single-cell data\n   - Integration points with other components\n\n2. Biological Considerations:\n   - How the architecture handles cell type specificity\n   - Incorporation of pathway knowledge\n   - Mechanisms for capturing perturbation effects\n   - Biological constraints and their implementation\n\n3. Technical Implementation:\n   - Detailed layer specifications\n   - Activation functions and their biological relevance\n   - Loss function design\n   - Training strategy recommendations\n\n4. Performance Optimization:\n   - Specific techniques to outperform SOTA\n   - Computational efficiency considerations\n   - Scalability solutions\n   - Memory optimization strategies\n\n5. Integration and Interpretability:\n   - Methods for model interpretation\n   - Visualization approaches\n   - Integration with existing biological knowledge\n   - Validation strategies\n\nFormat your response as a structured proposal with clear sections and specific technical details."
