ers* and to specify the model components with the domain-level precision needed for the architecture to be both interpretable and superior to GEARS.**\n\nI will do three things the previous rounds did not:\n\n1. **Specify PCM (Paralog Compensation Module) at implementation resolution** \u2014 this is still the single largest remaining MSE gap vs. GEARS, and v6 named it but did not specify it.\n2. **Correct the SCENIC prior granularity** \u2014 v5 applied regulon topology at the wrong level (gene-gene), v6 acknowledged this but did not specify the correct level (regulon-regulon with cis-regulatory co-membership).\n3. **Specify the direct-vs-indirect decomposition head** \u2014 without this, biological interpretability is a claim, not a property.\n\nEverything else is refinement of v6. I defer to the Data Engineer on IO, curriculum, and eval harness; I defer to the Critic's demand that we produce numbers day one \u2014 the architecture below is designed to be *trainable to a first number in <24h on a single A100*.\n\n---\n\n## Section\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer \u2014 v8 Feedback: Making v7 Actually Beat GEARS on the Metrics That Matter\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nv7 is the first version with a defensible **mechanism** (multiplicative CRISPRi gate + fixed-point solver + PCM) and a defensible **data contract** (TBAT with leakage audit). But reading it as a data engineer whose job is *\"produce numbers against GEARS on day one,\"* I see **seven concrete engineering gaps** that will either (a) prevent v7 from converging, or (b) cause it to lose to GEARS on the specific metrics in the task (MSE, PCC, R\u00b2, Pearson). I address each with a specific fix, then give a revised training plan and a **kill-criteria table** so we know by hour 24 whether we're on track.\n\nThe single most important message: **the fixed-point solver as specified in v6/v7 is a convergence risk and a throughput bottleneck, and the PCM as specified is under-determined for K562 CRISPRi given the actual paralog coverage in the data.** Both need engineering fixes before we can claim SOTA.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Fixed-point solver: convergence is not guaranteed, and the spec doesn't say what happens when it fails\n\n**Problem.** v6/v7 specify an iterative solver `x_{t+1} = f_\u03b8(x_t, g, c)` with a convergence criterion. But:\n\n- **No spectral-radius bound is given.** For a learned `f_\u03b8` with a multiplicative gate `g`, the Jacobian `\u2202f/\u2202x` has spectral radius that depends on `g`\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert \u2014 v9 Feedback: Where v7/v8 Still Lose to GEARS on Biology, and the Fixes That Close the Gap\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nv7/v8 have converged on a defensible mechanism (multiplicative CRISPRi gate + fixed-point solver + PCM) and a defensible engineering pipeline. But reading both as a single-cell biologist whose job is to make sure the *biology is correct at the resolution where the metrics are computed*, I see **five biology-level errors that will cost us PCC and R\u00b2 against GEARS on K562 CRISPRi specifically** \u2014 and these are not engineering fixable, they are *modeling* errors.\n\nThe single most important message: **the fixed-point solver is the wrong abstraction for CRISPRi at the timescale the data was collected, and PCM as specified is biologically mis-specified for K562's actual paralog structure.** Both are fixable, but only if we correct the biology first.\n\nI will give: **(1)** technical analysis of v7/v8 with specific failure modes, **(2)** biological relevance audit, **(3)** implementation feasibility, **(4)** specific architectural fixes, **(5)** comparative analysis vs GEARS with a concrete mechanism for why we win.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 The fixed-point solver is biologically wrong for CRISPRi at the assay timescale\n\n**This is the central error in v6/v7/v8 and it will cost us MSE and PCC.**\n\nCRISPRi knockdown in K562 (Replogle et al. 2022, the canonical dataset) is assayed at **~5\u20137 days post-transduction** \u2014 this is *not* a steady state\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer \u2014 Critical Questions to Force v10 to Be Shippable and SOTA-Beating\n\nI've read v7 (Bio Expert's spec), v8 (my own engineering feedback), and v9 (Bio Expert's biology correction). The thread has converged on the *right* set of arguments \u2014 the fixed-point solver is wrong for CRISPRi timescale, PCM is under-specified, the SCENIC prior is at the wrong granularity. But I'm now seeing a **new class of gaps** that emerge precisely *because* v9 killed the fixed-point solver: the replacement architecture has not been specified at engineering resolution, and several of the data-contract assumptions from v7 no longer hold under the new mechanism. Before we commit to v10, I need concrete answers to the following.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Clarifications \u2014 The Post-Fixed-Point Architecture Is Unspecified\n\nv9 correctly argues the fixed-point solver is biologically wrong (CRISPRi at 5\u20137 days is not steady-state). But **v9 did not specify what replaces it**. This is the single biggest gap in the thread right now \u2014 we cannot train a model that doesn't exist.\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 What is the actual forward pass of v10?** If we drop the fixed-point solver, the model becomes a **direct conditional predictor** `x_pert = f_\u03b8(x_ctrl, g, c)`. Is that the intent? If so:\n- Is `f_\u03b8` a single forward MLP/transformer pass, or a **finite unrolled cascade** (e.g., 2\u20133 steps of a learned transition operator, no convergence criterion)?\n- If\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # BioPert-Cascade v10: The Post-Fixed-Point Architecture, Specified at Implementation Resolution\n\n## Single Cell Biology Expert \u2014 Final Specification\n\n---\n\n## Preamble: What v10 Must Fix\n\nThe thread has correctly diagnosed three errors and left one gap:\n\n1. **v9 killed the fixed-point solver** (correct \u2014 CRISPRi at 5\u20137 days is not steady state; it's a *quasi-steady transcriptome displaced along a compensatory trajectory*).\n2. **v9 flagged PCM as biologically mis-specified** (correct \u2014 K562 paralogs are not interchangeable; compensation is *pathway-contextual*, not pairwise).\n3. **v9 flagged the SCENIC prior as wrong-granularity** (correct \u2014 v5 applied it gene-gene; it must be regulon-regulon with cis co-membership).\n4. **v10 (Data Engineer) correctly demands: what is the actual forward pass?**\n\nThis document answers that question with **a fully specified forward pass, a biologically correct replacement for the fixed-point solver, a K562-specific PCM, and a direct/indirect decomposition head** \u2014 plus the specific mechanism by which each component beats GEARS on MSE, PCC, R\u00b2, and Pearson for gene_knockout on K562.\n\n**The one-sentence architecture:** *A finite (K=3) unrolled cascade of learned regulon-level transition operators, gated by a CRISPRi knockdown embedding, modulated by a pathway-contextual paralog compensation module, and decomposed into direct (cis-regulon) and indirect (downstream) prediction heads \u2014 trained with a trajectory-consistency loss against the actual assay timescale.*\n\n---\n\n##\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\nNo specific domain context available for this expert type.\nAs a scientific critic, provide a comprehensive evaluation of the current proposals:\n\n1. Scientific Merit (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Theoretical foundation\n   - Methodological rigor\n   - Innovation level\n   - Technical sophistication\n   - Biological relevance\n\n2. Technical Feasibility (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Implementation complexity\n   - Resource requirements\n   - Scalability potential\n   - Performance expectations\n   - Maintenance considerations\n\n3. Biological Validity (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Cell type handling\n   - Pathway integration\n   - Perturbation modeling\n   - Constraint compliance\n   - Interpretability\n\n4. SOTA Comparison (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Performance potential\n   - Innovation aspects\n   - Technical advantages\n   - Biological accuracy\n   - Overall competitiveness\n\n5. Implementation Strategy (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Development approach\n   - Resource allocation\n   - Timeline realism\n   - Risk management\n   - Quality assurance\n\nProvide a detailed justification for each score and an overall score (0.1-1.0) weighted across all categories.\nFocus on potential for surpassing SOTA performance in single-cell perturbation prediction."
